  [1mThe fork:[0m the three options differ in how much of the work is [3mdiscovery[0m versus [3mconstruction[0m. "Real app elsewhere"
  means the task model, user model, and write paths are already decided and my job is to fit into them — cheap, but only
  if that app actually exists. "Prototype" means we build something convincing fast and throw away the architecture
  later. "Greenfield here" means we own all five pieces and the decisions are ours to make, at the cost of building the
  substrate before the feature you asked for.

  [1mMy recommendation: greenfield, built here, decomposed into stages.[0m Two reasons. The commit message "initial: empty
  tasks page" reads like a deliberate starting point rather than a stub in front of a real system — nothing here
  references an API, a build tool, or a framework, so there's no evidence of an app to fit into. And between greenfield
  and prototype, greenfield is the reversible choice: a small real task model can stay if the project continues, whereas
  prototype scaffolding gets deleted. If it turns out there [3mis[0m an app elsewhere, say the word and I'll read it instead
  — that would replace most of what follows.

  The consequence: [1mnotifications is the second thing we build, not the first.[0m The staging I'd propose is tasks + users +
  a write path first, then subscriptions + notifications on top. Each stage gets its own spec and plan. I'd brainstorm
  stage one with you now and keep the notifications design in view so stage one doesn't paint us into a corner — for
  instance, making sure task writes go through one chokepoint that can later emit events.

